  "answer_text": "Great question \u2014 and honestly, this is one of the internet's great pronunciation debates! Here's a breakdown:\n\n---\n\n## How to Pronounce \"Doge\" \ud83d\udc15\n\nAccording to Wikipedia, \"doge\" can be pronounced **/do\u028a\u0292/** (\"dohzh\"), **/do\u028ad\u0292/** (\"dohj\"), or **/do\u028a\u0261/** (\"dohg\"). Here are the main camps:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83c\udfc6 \"Dohj\" or \"Dohzh\" \u2014 The Most Widely Accepted\nThe pronunciation **\"dohj\"** (IPA: \u02c8d\u0259\u028a\u0292) was confirmed by Dogecoin's co-creator Jackson Palmer, who said the name was inspired by the animated series *Homestar Runner*. In one of the episodes, a character spells out D-O-G-E and another character reads it as \"dohj.\"\n\nThis pronunciation \u2014 a long O, soft G, and silent E \u2014 is widely believed to have originated with a Tumblr user who submitted a computer-read audio file to a site called \"Your Daily Doge,\" though it's also been attributed to that 2005 *Homestar Runner* episode.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \"Dohg\" \u2014 Like \"Rogue\" or \"Vogue\"\nSome people pronounce it with a long O, hard G, and silent E \u2014 like \"rogue\" \u2014 arguing that since the original word \"dog\" has a hard G, the silent E only modifies the vowel, not the consonant, similar to how \"slop\" becomes \"slope.\"\n\n---\n\n### 3. \"Doh-geh\" \u2014 Two Syllables\nThere's also \"Doh-geh,\" with a long O, hard G, and a spoken \"e\" sound at the end, somewhat like the end of \"ballet.\" Some argue this sounds like a quirky version of \"doggy.\"\n\n---\n\n### 4. \"Dog\" or \"Doggy\" \u2014 Literal\nSome people just say it like the animal. This is the most literal reading but the least common among internet culture enthusiasts.\n\n---\n\n### The Verdict\nThe most widely cited \"correct\" pronunciation is **\"dohj\"** (IPA: \u02c8d\u0259\u028a\u0292). However, as with GIF, both the hard G (like dog) and the soft G are in common use and can be justified with reasonable explanations.\n\nSo if you want to sound like an internet native, go with **\"dohj\"** \u2014 but no matter what you choose, you'll have company! Much pronunciation. Very debate. Wow. \ud83d\udc15",
